"""Correct existing acquisition funding claims; no new URL or 509 credit."""
import re,subprocess
from refresh_dscr_purchase_methods import refresh
from expand_hold_period import ROOT

BODY='''<p class="lead">A cleared tax refund can contribute to an investor's next STR purchase, but a projected deduction cannot fund today's deposit or closing. The “refund loop” is a conditional capital-redeployment idea, not a self-funding acquisition program. Before pursuing a second property, obtain a CPA-reviewed tax case, keep the first property's obligations funded and test the next purchase without the expected refund. The useful question is how much money is actually available, on which date, for which commitment.</p>
<p><a href="/apply/">Bring the next-property cash schedule and no-refund case to an acquisition call</a>. BNB Accelerator is an acquisition service provider, not a CPA firm. Tax conclusions belong with a qualified adviser; our independent partner is <a href="https://aetaxadvisors.com/short-term-rentals/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">AE Tax Advisors</a>.</p>
<h2>Separate a deduction, a tax reduction and a cash refund</h2>
<p>A cost-segregation estimate concerns asset classification and depreciation timing; it is not the amount of tax refunded. Ask the CPA to compare the supported depreciation case with the appropriate alternative, apply the buyer's actual limitations and estimate the change in tax payable. Then identify whether any resulting benefit is a refund, reduced future payments or no currently usable cash. Do not apply one headline marginal rate to every estimated deduction and call the result a guaranteed deposit.</p>
<p>A reduction in tax payable and a refund are not interchangeable. A refund depends on the actual return and payments already made, among other facts. If withholding or estimated payments were already reduced and the resulting cash has been spent, do not count that same benefit again as a future refund. The <a href="/blog/str-tax-benefit-shortfall-purchase/">smaller-or-delayed-benefit guide</a> addresses the property's sensitivity; this page connects cleared proceeds from an earlier activity to a new acquisition.</p>
<h2>Obtain the eligibility review before making a funding promise</h2>
<p><a href="https://www.irs.gov/publications/p925">IRS Publication 925</a> distinguishes rental-activity exceptions, material participation and loss limitations. An average customer-use period of seven days or less is one exception; another involves thirty days or less and significant personal services. Exceeding seven days does not by itself establish the opposite tax result. A short-stay label and participation do not bypass basis, at-risk or potentially applicable excess-business-loss limits.</p>
<p>Have the CPA review actual ownership, use, services, participation evidence, basis, readiness and applicable depreciation rules. No deduction or refund is computed here. A larger purchase price, high W-2 income or an owner's selected management tasks does not establish a usable benefit. Do not change an operating representation or invent work hours to fit an illustration.</p>
<p>Publication 925 also describes grouping based on an appropriate economic unit, with restrictions and disclosure requirements. It can affect participation measurement and disposition treatment. There is no automatic third-property threshold or universal election that makes every portfolio eligible. Review grouping with the CPA before relying on it for a new acquisition; the actual facts and reporting requirements control.</p>
<h2>Use a capital-redeployment register</h2>
<div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Question</th><th>Evidence to obtain</th><th>Purchase consequence</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>What tax effect is supported?</td><td>CPA comparison, assumptions and unresolved limitations</td><td>Do not use the gross deduction as available cash</td></tr>
<tr><td>What money has actually arrived?</td><td>Cleared receipt and a reconciliation of earlier reduced payments</td><td>Count the same benefit once, only when available</td></tr>
<tr><td>What must remain with the first property?</td><td>Supported operating, debt, repair and reserve commitments</td><td>Do not fund the next down payment by quietly spending the first property's buffer</td></tr>
<tr><td>What is due for the next property?</td><td>Dated deposit, closing, service, setup and reserve allocation</td><td>Resolve early invoices independently of a later receipt</td></tr>
<tr><td>What if no refund arrives?</td><td>No-receipt budget and genuinely available funding alternatives</td><td>Delay, change the candidate or decline an unfunded commitment</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p>Record the source, amount, availability date and assigned use of each dollar. Keep household commitments separate from property capital. A submitted return, expected payment or bank screenshot without context does not establish the full spendable balance. Share private tax and bank records only through agreed secure channels; the acquisition team needs the bounded funding conclusion, not publicly disclosed returns.</p>
<h2>Worked purchase comparison: an expected refund cannot close the gap</h2>
<p>Invented arithmetic only, not tax advice, a client outcome, lender terms or a predicted refund. Assume $260,000 of cleared cash. Preserve $50,000 for the first property's independently chosen obligations and buffer and $40,000 for household commitments, leaving $170,000 for the next acquisition. These retained amounts are allocations, not additional expenses or money that can also fund the new purchase.</p>
<p>The new property requires a hypothetical $150,000 down payment, $15,000 distinct closing costs, $25,000 setup and $35,000 new-property retained reserve: $225,000 total. No acquisition-service fee or omitted bill is assumed included; add any actual distinct amount. Without a new receipt, the stated purchase has a $55,000 funding gap. The property's revenue projection cannot supply that missing closing cash.</p>
<p>Suppose an entirely invented $60,000 refund is expected but has not cleared. Available new-purchase cash remains $170,000, not $230,000. If a $10,000 deposit is paid from that cash and credited against the $150,000 down payment, remaining cash is $160,000 and the remaining allocation is $215,000. The gap is still $55,000. Do not charge the credited deposit twice, assume it is recoverable or waive transaction protections to wait for a tax payment.</p>
<p>If the actual $60,000 receipt later clears and is freely available, remaining cash becomes $220,000 against the $215,000 remaining allocation, leaving $5,000. If only $30,000 clears, the remaining cash is $190,000 and the gap is $25,000. If nothing clears, the original $55,000 gap remains. These scenarios do not establish that the tax outcome is caused by the property or that funds will arrive before contract deadlines.</p>
<p>In the full-receipt case, a distinct $8,000 repair not already included in setup raises the remaining allocation to $223,000, creating a $3,000 gap against $220,000. The original $35,000 reserve remains protected inside the allocation; consuming it changes the risk plan rather than proving funding is adequate. Use the <a href="/tools/first-str-purchase-budget/">complete purchase-budget worksheet</a> to add actual fees and sequence the bills.</p>
<h2>Evaluate the next property independently and keep the exit file</h2>
<p>The source of capital does not validate the new address, permit, lender approval or seller revenue. Compare supported operations and downside, then ask whether two properties create simultaneous repair, vacancy or personal-work obligations. A prior purchase or a published case study does not prove another buyer can reproduce a pace, funding source or tax result. This article does not attribute a client's expansion to refunds or infer that operating cash could not have funded it.</p>
<p><a href="https://www.irs.gov/publications/p551">IRS Publication 551</a> explains reductions to basis for depreciation allowed or allowable. <a href="https://www.irs.gov/publications/p544">Publication 544</a> describes depreciation recapture and different asset treatments on disposition. Do not label every benefit permanent, every sale receipt taxable gain or every prior deduction automatically recaptured at one rate. Retain actual asset records and obtain a CPA-reviewed hold/sale comparison; the <a href="/blog/cost-seg-timing-and-hold-period/">hold-period worksheet</a> addresses that separate decision.</p>
<h2>Choose a funded next step</h2>
<p>Proceed only when the next acquisition fits verified capital, permission, financing and downside without needing an unreceived tax payment. Reprice or choose a different candidate when actual costs change a viable plan. Delay under valid protection if decisive funding evidence remains unresolved; counsel determines notice, deposit and extension rights. Decline an unfunded purchase rather than force a “loop” by borrowing without accepted terms or draining protected cash.</p>
<p><a href="/apply/">Discuss a property-based offer ceiling and dated funding register</a>. The <a href="/blog/buy-str-high-income-large-tax-bill/">high-income buyer roadmap</a> connects capital to the rest of the purchase journey. A call can examine contracted acquisition support; it cannot certify tax eligibility, promise a refund or guarantee an investment result.</p>
<p class="small">IRS primary publications reviewed October 7, 2026; the linked editions are currently published 2025 materials, not newly issued 2026 editions. Educational only, not personalized tax, legal, lending or investment advice. All example amounts are hypothetical. No refund amount, timing, deduction, financing, portfolio scale or return is guaranteed.</p>'''
FAQ=[
('Can a tax refund fund the next rental property down payment?', 'A cleared, freely available refund can contribute after existing property and household obligations are funded. A projected deduction or expected refund is not closing cash; have a CPA establish the tax case and test the next purchase without that receipt.'),
('What stops the refund loop from working?', 'An unusable or smaller tax effect, a later receipt, already-spent reduced tax payments, protected existing obligations or a new-property cash gap can prevent reinvestment. Seven-day averages alone do not settle eligibility or all loss limitations.'),
('How many properties can this scale to?', 'No universal property count is established. Review independent capital, financing, operational downside, actual participation and any appropriate grouping with qualified advisers. A published example is not proof of repeatable refunds or acquisition pace.')]
